#  Ghurify API - manual checks
#
#  Open this file in Visual Studio (or VS Code with the REST Client extension) and click
#  "Send Request" above any request below.
#
#  Start the API first:   dotnet run --project src/Ghurify.Api
#
#  The one-time code is NOT returned by the API. With Email:UserName and Email:Password set
#  it is emailed for real. Without them, Development writes it to the API console as:
#      DEVELOPMENT EMAIL: the sign-in code for r****i@example.com is 123456.
#  That only happens in Development with no mail account; no other environment logs the code.

@host = http://localhost:5199
@email = rizvi@example.com

# Paste the six digits from the API console here before sending "2. Verify".
@code = 000000


### 1. Health - should be 200 with both statuses "Healthy"
GET {{host}}/api/v1/health
Accept: application/json


### 2. Request a one-time code - should be 202 Accepted
# Response gives expiresInSeconds (300) and resendAfterSeconds (60).
# Now look at the API console for the code, and put it in @code above.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/otp
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}"
}


### 3. Verify the code - should be 200
# Look at the response headers: Set-Cookie carries ghurify_rt with HttpOnly and
# path=/api/v1/auth. The refresh token is NOT in the body, only the access token.
# Copy the accessToken value into @token below to use request 5.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/verify
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "code": "{{code}}"
}


### Paste the accessToken from request 3 here.
@token = paste-access-token-here


### 4. Who am I, WITHOUT a token - should be 401
GET {{host}}/api/v1/auth/whoami
Accept: application/json


### 5. Who am I, WITH the token - should be 200 and a masked email address
GET {{host}}/api/v1/auth/whoami
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 6. Who am I, with a forged token - should be 401
GET {{host}}/api/v1/auth/whoami
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer not.a.real.jwt


### ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Things that SHOULD fail. Each one proves a rule is being enforced.
### ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


### 7. An address that is not an email - should be 400 with a validation problem
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/otp
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "not-an-email"
}


### 8. A wrong code - should be 401, and the message must not say WHY it failed
# Send this five times: the code locks, and after that even the correct code is refused.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/verify
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "code": "000001"
}


### 9. A code for an address that never requested one - should be 401, identical to request 8
# Identical responses are deliberate: a different answer would reveal which addresses are
# registered on the platform.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/verify
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "never-used@example.com",
  "code": "123456"
}


### 10. Rate limit - send this FOUR times in a row for one address.
# The first three are 202. The fourth is 429: three codes per address per ten minutes,
# counted in the database so the limit survives restarts and extra API instances.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/otp
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "ratelimit@example.com"
}


### ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Session lifetime. Requests 11-13 use the cookie the browser/VS stored from request 3.
### ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


### 11. Refresh - should be 200 and set a NEW ghurify_rt cookie
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/refresh


### 12. Refresh again straight away - should be 200 again (the new cookie rotates too)
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/refresh


### 13. Sign out - should be 204, and revokes the whole token family
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/logout


### 14. Refresh after signing out - should be 401
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/refresh


### 15. The OpenAPI document the React client is generated from
GET {{host}}/openapi/v1.json
